	//
	// Binds a public key to an identity name and, when the name is new, creates the identity and subscribes it to the general channel at the current head. The server's registration setting decides, never the request: open (trust on first use, the default on a loopback or Unix-socket listener) lets anyone claim a name that has no credential, and enrollment (the default on any other listener) requires an enrollment token, which a host administrator creates. A keyless administrator row (after reset-credential) needs an enrollment token even in open mode. A name that already has a credential cannot take another key (409 conflict; a lost credential is recovered with the host command reset-credential), a scoped identity registers only with the enrollment token bound to it, and a human identity cannot register (403). Registering the same key again changes nothing, consumes no token and answers success with created false, so a client whose response was lost can retry. Open operation. The caller has no credential yet. Side effects: creates the identity and its key; consumes one use of the enrollment token when one is given. No idempotency key: receipts are per identity and the caller has none yet, and a repeat with the same key is safe by construction. Source: spec 4.2, Decisions 20, 58, 72.
